><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250604094900+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250604094902+10'00'" name="40416375-0363-4828-80b1-41bd20c654d4" page="8" coords="306.008026,507.177917,543.021118,507.177917,306.008026,495.501953,543.021118,495.501953,306.008026,494.199799,543.004639,494.046509,306.008026,482.523865,543.004639,482.370544,306.028290,481.068390,543.026123,481.068390,306.028290,469.392456,543.026123,469.392456,306.028290,468.027283,542.962952,468.027283,306.028290,456.351318,542.962952,456.351318,306.028290,455.049164,543.015564,455.049164,306.028290,443.373230,543.015564,443.373230,306.028290,442.071014,543.047302,442.071014,306.028290,430.395081,543.047302,430.395081,306.028290,429.029907,542.919128,429.029907,306.028290,417.353943,542.919128,417.353943,306.028290,416.051758,542.973267,416.051758,306.028290,404.375793,542.973267,404.375793,306.028290,403.073639,543.037720,403.073639,306.028290,391.397705,543.037720,391.397705,306.028290,390.032532,542.942139,390.032532,306.028290,378.356567,542.942139,378.356567,306.028290,377.054413,542.980774,377.054413,306.028290,365.378479,542.980774,365.378479,306.028290,364.076294,543.024048,364.076294,306.028290,352.400330,543.024048,352.400330,306.028290,351.035156,543.010132,351.035156,306.028290,339.359192,543.010132,339.359192,306.028290,338.057037,542.883301,338.057037,306.028290,326.381104,542.883301,326.381104,306.028290,325.078918,449.532349,325.078918,306.028290,313.402954,449.532349,313.402954" rect="305.643127,313.038086,543.412170,507.542786" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: These results may be useful in the daily routine of coaches&#xD;and conditioning instructors to monitor training adaptations&#xD;throughout the judo training process, especially the&#xD;relative maximum strength and the fatigue index in the&#xD;endurance test. In addition, the tests applied in our&#xD;research could be useful in talent identification in judo,&#xD;since they were effective to discriminate athletes from nonathletes.&#xD;Use of coactivation as a measure tool could collaborate&#xD;in strength-endurance performance assessment,&#xD;showing peripherical evidence of motor skill development.&#xD;Beyond that, isometric endurance handgrip test could be&#xD;part of performance monitoring in a strength and conditioning&#xD;training that can be performed beyond conventional&#xD;judo sessions, especially training periodization that&#xD;aims to develop handgrip function.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="8" rect="593.971985,415.177917,777.971985,507.177917"
/></highlight
